  "strategy": "**Approach and rationale**  \nWe are minimizing the autocorrelation peak ratio \\( J(a) = \\frac{2n \\max(\\text{convolve}(a, a))}{(\\operatorname{sum}(a))^2} \\).  \nThe key bottlenecks in the previous approach were:**  \n- Limited exploration across longer sequence lengths.**  \n- Ineffective use of linear programming (LP) for autocorrelation optimization.**  \n- Slow convergence due to excessive iterations.**  \n\nOur new approach combines:  \n1. **Adaptive dimension expansion** via interpolation to minimize re-computation while maintaining structure.  \n2. **Physics-informed optimization** leveraging adjoint methods to compute gradients efficiently.  \n3. **Targeted descent methods** with controlled momentum and line search to speed up convergence.  \n\nWe treat sequences as functions on \\([0, 1]\\) with autocorrelation constraints in frequency domain, enabling direct gradient-based optimization.**  \n\n**Mathematical specification**  \n- **Objective**: Lower bound on \\( J(a) \\).  \n- **Representation**: Unit-sum sequences of integer length \\( n \\) generated from normalized samples:  \n  \\[\n  a_i = \\left( \\sum_{k=0}^{n} |c_k| \\right)^{-1} \\sum_{k=0}^{n} |c_k| \\cdot \\phi(t_k, t_i)\n  \\]  \n  where \\(\\phi(t_k, t_i)\\) is a Gaussian kernel function \\[\n  \\phi(t_k, t_i) = \\exp\\left(-\\frac{(t_i - t_k)^2}{2\\sigma^2}\\right), \\sigma = 0.1\n  \\]  \n- **Gradients**: Use adjoint method from the autocorrelation constraint \\(\\|\\text{convolve}(a, a)\\|_\\infty \\le M\\), equivalent to enforcing:  \n  \\[\n  \\max_{k=0,\\dots,2n-2} |(a * a)_k| \\le M\n  \\]  \n- **Constraints**: Nonnegative entries, aggregated sum at least 0.01.  \n\n**Implementation procedure**  \n```python\nimport numpy as np\nfrom scipy.optimize import minimize\nfrom scipy.sparse import diags\n\ndef evaluate_sequence(seq):\n    # ... existing function from original task\n\ndef autocorrelation_constraint(a, t=None):\n    # Compute convolution\n    conv = np.convolve(a, a, mode=\"full\")\n    max_val = np.max(conv)\n    s = np.sum(a)\n    return 2 * len(a) * max_val / (s ** 2) if s != 0 else np.inf\n\ndef build_adaptive_initialization(parent_len=512, target_init=\"sine_wave\"):\n    \"\"\"Generate diverse initialization sequences beyond the parent solution.\"\"\"\n    def autotune_time():\n        \"\"\"Estimate time costs for hybrid method.\"\"\"\n        # Placeholder for time estimation subroutines that measure FFT & LP scaling factors\n        pass\n\n    n = 1024  # Starting length beyond parent\n    base_budget = 400  # Seconds per major optimization step\n    budget_remaining = 1500  # Total budget\n\n    if budget_remaining > 600:\n        # Keep parent as base solution, try expanding to 1024 & 2048\n        # Represent parent sequence as a time-domain function via Gaussian process\n        base_func = interpolate_time_domain(parent_seq)          \n        n_target = [1024, 2048]\n        # Blended interpolation from parent function for new dimensions\n        for n in n_target:\n            if budget_remaining <= 0: break\n            # Use time-frequency duality to project constraints into known spaces\n            # Sample Base Function\n            t_points = np.linspace(0.001, 1-0.001, n)\n            candidate = base_func(t_points)\n            # Early-bound expectation using parent's J\n            expectation_J = 1.48 + 0.03 * (n / parent_len)  # Lower bound estimate\n            if expectation_J < 1.5:  # Only pursue if likely to find improvement\n                try:\n                    # Apply hybrid_optimizer from parent method\n                    # Reduced shots due to interpolation leveraging parent solution\n                    result = gradient_free_search(candidate, budget=base_budget)\n                    if evaluation_improves(parent, result):\n                        parent = result  # Update parent reference\n                except Exception as e:\n                    pass  # Fallback to existing if solver fails\n\n        # Post-processing to clean up the solution\n        final = denormalize_solution(parent)  # Ensure original integer solution is recovered\n\n    elif budget_remaining > 200 and parent_len <= 500:\n        # If time is moderate, find a better candidate at the original parent length\n        # Use improved learning rate scheduling and new descent method\n        result = new_coordinate_descent(parent)\n        final = denormalize_solution(result)\n\n    else:  # Budget too tight or sequence is small\n        # Fast hybrid override for small sequences\n        final = standard_hybrid(parent, time_out=20)  # Limited iterations (20)\n\n    return final\n\ndef gradient_free_search(a, budget):\n    \"\"\"Optimize a sequence using hybrid methods focused on acceleration.\"\"\"\n    # This function implements the forward-backward operator approach known from the previous program\n    # It provides an estimate of new designs but uses a cpu-friendly variation\n    initial = np.array(a)\n    f = lambda x: (2 * len(x) * np.max(np.convolve(x, x, mode=\"full\")) \n                  - (np.sum(x) if np.sum(x) > 0 else 1)**2  # Penalizing tiny aggregates\n    df = lambda x: ...  # Compute finite-difference gradient using adjacent convolutions\n\n    # Combined minimization\n    res = minimize(f, x0, method=\"L-BFGS-B\", jac=df, bounds=[(0, 100) for _ in range(len(initial))])\n    if res.success:\n        scaled = res.x / np.sum(res.x)  # Normalization critical here\n        return clip_and_normalize(scaled)\n    else:\n        # Fallback to brute-force if gradient not available\n        return brute_force_enhanced(a, budget)\n```\n\n**Search choices and useful variations**  \n- **Initialization Quality**: By default, generate high-quality interpolations, but for very limited time, use fallback hybrids.  \n- **Dimension Scalability**:\n  - Use interpolation initially for 1024/2048 sequences  \n  - Bound optimization for targeted sampling at 700 to 2500 points (instead of FFT expenses)  \n- **Gradient Computing**: Use analytic differentiation for known spaces, fall back to finite difference if conditions unknown.  \n\n**Computation budget and robustness**  \nTime limits per task:  \n- Minimal total execution time: 600 seconds  \n- Graceful degradation: Can handle up to 250 seconds on shared hardware  \n- Processed conditions: Never freeze for optimization steps  \n- Fallback initialization: Gaussian mixtures with peaks limited to 0.5 sample periods  \n\n**Validation and return contract**  \n- Public summary: \"Advanced interpolation-based hybrid optimizer\"  \n- All output sequences must pass \\( evaluate\\_sequence \\) checks  \n- Returns the best found sequence.",
